==Latin==
{{attention|la}}

===Etymology===
From  {{proto|Indo-European|dṓm|lang=la}}, from root {{proto|Indo-European|demh₂-|to build|lang=la|title=}}. Cognates include Ancient Greek {{term|δόμος|sc=polytonic|tr=dómos|lang=grc}} and Sanskrit {{term|दम|tr=dáma|lang=sa|sc=Deva}}. The same [[Proto-Indo-European]] root also gave Old English {{term|timber||building, act of building|lang=ang}} (English {{term|timber|lang=en}}).

===Noun===
'''domus''' (''genitive'' '''domūs''' or '''[[domi|domī]]'''); {{f}}, ''[[Appendix:Latin fourth declension|fourth declension]]'' (irregular)

# [[house]], [[home]]

====Usage notes====
'''Domus''' is one of three common nouns that take the [[locative case]], the other two being [[rus#Latin|rus]] and [[humus#Latin|humus]]. It is irregular in that it has a mix of second and fourth declension forms, the second declension forms being more commonly used in place constructions.

